I've been using the O-Scope now for over 4 years. I use it daily; it's almost always around my neck and on most days it gets more use than my stethoscope. I have a lot to say about the O-Scope, so I pulled it (and its accessories) out of the ◊link["/blog/whats-in-my-bag"]{What's in My Bag} post to give it its own post.

◊section[#:id "what-is-the-o-scope"]{What is the O-Scope?}
The VorOtek O-Scope is an examination tool invented by Melbourne otorhinolaryngologist Dr John Vorrath. Most likely you've seen one hanging around the neck of your local ◊sc{ENT} registrar / fellow. To understand what it is it's perhaps better to start by understanding what it isn't: it's not a pair of loupes. The O-Scope has +2 dioptre lenses, but these are just for working distance---there is no appreciable magnification. So what does it do? 3 things:

◊list{
- 1. Significantly reduce your interpupillary distance (◊sc{IPD}).
- 2. Provide a coaxial light source.
- 3. Free your hands.
}

Why a reduced ◊sc{IPD}? It lets you look down long, narrow passages (ears, nose, throat) while retaining binocular vision. The company has a marketing figure that makes it a bit clearer:
◊figure["img/vorotek-promotional.jpg" #:id "vorotek-promotional-graphic" #:title "VorOtek Promotional Graphic" #:alt "A VorOtek diagram showing how bringing the two eyes closer together lets both look straight down a narrow passage such as the ear canal." #:licence "public-domain" #:credit "https://www.vorotek.com/products/o-scope" #:width 0.5]{Why reducing ◊sc{IPD} helps.}

A coaxial light source is critical for this: if the beam of light isn't coaxial with your line of sight then shadows are a problem. So what does this look like in practice? Wearing the O-Scope, I can stand 15--20cm away from a kid's ear, gently manipulate the pinna, and be staring straight at the ear drum---with a good appreciation of its position thanks to binocular (◊sc{3D}) vision. In some kids (with straight noses, small turbinates, and no boogers) I can see their nasopharynx from the front. And my view of the throat is excellent. These are useful things, but we can already do them (albeit less gracefully) with a traditional otoscope. It's the last point---freeing your hands---that will change your practice.

For years it bothered me that examining the ear drum is a critically important part of the paediatric examination---unless, of course, there's wax in the way. With the O-Scope, the right tools, and the proper technique, removing wax to see the drum is generally a trivial matter (I talk more about this under ◊link["#o-scope-accessories"]{Accessories}). Have you ever tried to swab a tonsil and realised that you're one hand short (tongue depressor, penlight, swab...)? Easy with the O-Scope. Even simpler than that: when looking in the throat you can gently hold the kid's head with both hands and move it around until you get the best view. Or you can get fancy: insert a mirror into the oropharynx for looking up (indirect retrograde rhinoscopy) or down (indirect laryngoscopy).

Hand-in-hand with my frustrations about giving up on otoscopy in the presence of wax has been my general frustration with the quality of otoscopy skill and interpretation. As paediatricians we should be second only to ◊sc{ENT} surgeons when it comes to otoscopy, but I don't think that is universally true. How many times as a registrar did I examine a kid in ◊sc{ED} with fever and an "ear infection" when the drum was actually just hyperaemic? All the time! Lots of things can make an ear drum red, not just acute otitis media. A crying child, a febrile child, a child with myringitis. The diagnosis of ◊sc{AOM} isn't based on the colour of the drum, but the presence of symptoms in the setting of a middle ear effusion (typically with a drum that is bulging). An effusion, however, is the ◊em{sine qua non} of ◊sc{AOM}: if there isn't fluid, there isn't ◊sc{AOM}. ◊link["#pneumatic-otoscopy"]{Pneumatic otoscopy} allows you to test that, in much the same way as with a tympanogram.

The O-Scope makes pneumatic otoscopy very easy.◊aside{Dr Vorrath made a great educational video on using the O-Scope for pneumatic otoscopy ◊link["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YzhspnatKlg"]{here}.} I use pneumatic otoscopy every time I examine an ear.

◊subsection[#:id "pneumatic-otoscopy"]{Pneumatic otoscopy.}
I'm sure that many readers will be very familiar with pneumatic otoscopy (probably have been doing it for longer than I've been alive!); you can skip this part. That said, there are probably far more who (like me) trained in a place and time where the skill of pneumatic otoscopy had become a dead art form. Maybe some of you didn't even notice that your otoscope has a little nipple for attaching a bulb. Allow me to enlighten you!

In pneumatic otoscopy you form a seal around the ear canal and observe the tympanic membrane while changing the air pressure on this side of the drum. If the middle ear is aerated then the drum should be compliant---it'll move. If there is liquid in the middle ear then the drum won't move (liquids are functionally incompressible). That's an oversimplification of course. You can have bubbles in the middle ear with some drum movement, but you can usually see bubbles and they prove the presence of liquid. In Eustachian tube dysfunction you can have middle ear pressure that is negative relative to atmospheric. The drum will be retracted (sucked back) and can be quite taut. When you apply positive pressure to the canal the drum doesn't really move; when you apply negative pressure the drum moves back towards you. Pneumatic otoscopy takes a lot of practice (because you have to teach yourself), but it's a fun and rewarding experience.

◊section[#:id "buying-an-o-scope"]{Buying an O-Scope}
I bought my O-Scope directly from ◊link["https://www.vorotek.com/"]{VorOtek} in 2022; it cost me ◊sc{AUD}$2,400. They don't have an online store, so you're dealing with a rep. There are a couple of decisions you need to make when you buy one, and I have strong opinions on what you should choose!

◊list{
- You can choose to have the O-Scope mounted on spectacles or on a headband. You should absolutely choose the former. The headband is bulky (so you won't want to take it with you) and awkward to put on (so you won't want to use it). The headband is good for people who will be operating while wearing the O-Scope, but that isn't us. It's also good for when you want to buy one for the department (◊strong{don't do that}---◊link["#personal-departmental"]{see below}).
- You can choose to buy the O-Scope on its own or in a kit. Get the kit! It comes with some useful accessories (◊link["#o-scope-accessories"]{see below}) as well as a nice case. I lug my kit all over Sydney, so the case is too bulky for me, but it is nice to have.
- On your side of the O-Scope the windows need to match your ◊sc{IPD}. You can either have that measured by an optometrist and have them made to measure, or you can get the adjustable ones. Get the custom ones. I first used an O-Scope in an ◊sc{ED}; it was adjustable, and I was always second-guessing whether I had the measurement right. I also found the build quality a little chintzy: why have more moving parts that can cause a problem in the future? Finally, having one custom-made for your ◊sc{IPD} is a strong disincentive to share it: ◊strong{don't share it} (see below).
- You can (or at least you used to be able to) buy the O-Scope in either +2 or +3 dioptre versions. This comes down to working distance. I think I got the +2 version. It's worth checking what would be best with the rep.
}

◊anchor["personal-departmental"]So, should you buy one for yourself or one for the department? Again, I have strong feelings about this---buy one for yourself! Here's why:

◊list{
- Using the O-Scope is a skill that requires practice. I use mine every day because looking in the ears, nose, and throat is important when considering why kids are having a hard time. But it's also for the practice. Having a repertoire of hundreds of "normal" examinations is really helpful when something isn't normal (like a cholesteatoma). If you've read this far in such a niche post you might just be geeky enough to commit to that practice; many won't have that temperament. Your heart might skip a beat when you diagnose a retracted drum from a foot away without even reaching for the insufflator. You may then find that your colleagues couldn't give a hoot about such things! Also: the way to make the most of it is to have it around your neck. You won't do that if you feel obliged to share!
- The O-Scope isn't flimsy... but I wouldn't call it robust. If it's your baby you'll look after it; your colleagues---maybe not so much. (And registrars? Forgetaboutit!)
- Worst case scenario: someone who doesn't know what they're doing thinks they can put on the O-Scope and dig some wax out of a kid's ear. That won't end well.
}

So my recommendation: buy your own and wear it with pride!

◊section[#:id "o-scope-accessories"]{O-Scope accessories}
The O-Scope kit includes some useful accessories, mostly for otoscopy. I furnished mine with a few other things that I'll also list here.

◊subsection[#:id "battery-and-charger"]{Battery and charger.}
Hardly an accessory, but I'll put it here. The battery clips to your belt and plugs in to the cable that runs from the O-Scope. While I use mine every day, the light isn't actually on for very long each time. I've gone ◊em{months} without charging it. One thing to note: it doesn't have a rheostat, so there is only one light intensity---you can't dim it. The main implication: the O-Scope isn't fantastic for looking at things right next to the eye!

◊subsection[#:id "pneumatic-otoscope"]{Pneumatic otoscope.}
This is simple: a +6 dioptre lens attached to an insufflation bulb by a flexible tube. There's also an o-ring to form a seal with the speculum. For the most part it's fine---it works beautifully. I have 3 critiques though:

◊list{
- In my opinion the tubing is too long. In the video linked above in which Dr Vorrath demonstrates pneumatic otoscopy he holds the otoscope and the pinna with one hand while squeezing the bulb with the other. That doesn't work for me, for 2 reasons: Firstly, I find it awkward (and the kids don't like it). Secondly, the drum is often retracted; if you apply positive pressure to a retracted drum you don't really see much---you have to apply negative pressure. If the bulb is un-squeezed when you create a seal then you can't apply negative pressure. For that reason I like to have the bulb partially squeezed when I form a seal and observe the drum's response to negative pressure first, then positive pressure. That's easiest to do with the otoscope held and squeezed with one hand. Hard to explain, see ◊link["#modified-pneumatic-otoscope"]{below}. There is a simple fix: cut the tubing back to a length of 2cm. You don't need to do it yourself, get them to make the modification before they send it out. Makes a huge difference!
- You can't open it. If the inner surface for the lens gets grummy it isn't easy to clean. Try to keep it clean, don't go sticking your fingers in there!◊aside{If you need to clean it then some alcohol on a Q-tip works fine.}
- The whole thing feels a little chintzy. It's not expensive, so doesn't matter. But I'd much prefer it if it was machined out of brass, maybe with a screw-on lens to access for cleaning. If I ever find someone with a metal lathe I'll measure it up and see if it's feasible... until then, just look after it!
}

◊figure["img/pneumatic-modified.jpg" #:id "modified-pneumatic-otoscope" #:title "Modified pneumatic otoscope" #:alt "A pneumatic otoscope with its tubing cut back to about two centimetres, leaving the bulb close to the otoscope body." #:licence "own" #:width 0.5]{Tubing shortened to allow single-handed use.}

◊subsection[#:id "specula"]{Specula.}
Technically these are Gruber's specula, and they are orders of magnitude better than the 2.5mm disposable Welch Allyn ones you might be familiar with. Here's why:

◊list{
- The aperture is an oval, not a circle. This just means that you get a better fit to the anatomy of the canal, which you need for effective pneumatic otoscopy. The only reason I put this first is so that the next point makes sense.
- They are much larger. They come in sizes: XS, S, M, L, and XL. I measured them (maximum diameter, remember: it's an oval) so you don't have to: XS=2.5mm; S=3.5mm; M=4.5mm; L=5.5mm; XL=7mm. For reference, the little black disposable Welch Allyn specula that are ubiquitous (or always missing) in hospitals come in 2.5mm and 4mm versions. By far the most common is the 2.5mm, which is tiny! I mostly find myself using the L and XL specula; I rarely use the S and I haven't used the XS. That means I am getting a much bigger view of the drum and I'm not having to stick a pointy bit of plastic as deep into the canal. Also, I can almost always get a good seal with just the speculum---no proprietary little rubber things here!◊aside{Sometimes the canal is weird and I can't get a good seal; for those cases I carry some Blu Tack, but I rarely need it.}
- They are reusable. In the kit you get 3 of each size. I put them in a little divided box and it has been all I've needed. No carrying hundreds of little specula that fall out in your bag and get everywhere! The website says they are autoclavable, but to me that's overkill. I'm never instrumenting through them, the only body fluid is cerumen. I just clean them in and out with Tuffy wipes. You can tear a strip of wipe and thread it through the speculum, it works really well.
- They are designed to plug onto the otoscope and form a seal with the o-ring.
}

◊subsection[#:id "instruments-for-getting-things-out-of-ears"]{Instruments for getting things out of ears.}
The kit comes with three tools: 2 wax rings / curettes, 2 right-angle hooks, and a wool carrier. They are made of metal and seem to be of excellent quality. I use the curettes and the hooks frequently for wax removal, and from time to time I have pulled foreign bodies out of ears (incidental finding in a developmental consultation). I don't use the wool carrier much, but it has been useful when I wasn't sure if I was looking at otitis externa on its own or otitis externa secondary to chronic suppurative otitis media. Few things in life give as much satisfaction as patiently removing pus from an ear to reveal a perforation!

If you're going to use these then you really need to know what you're doing. I was lucky to have a very hands-on rural ◊sc{ENT} term in my final year of medical school where my job was to pull rubbish out of the ears of Ballarat sheep farmers! For those who didn't get to have that much fun Dr Vorrath made a very good video that explains it ◊link["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bdUBsdNhgbA"]{here}. The principles are as follows:

◊list{
- Learn to be ambidextrous. Use the right hand for the right ear and the left hand for the left ear.
- Always stabilise against the head: assume that the head is going to suddenly and violently move towards you and your pointy stick! If the stick moves with the head then it'll be ok.
- Only instrument under direct vision. The O-Scope makes this easy, but there are lots of times when the thing you'd like to get out is too close to the drum---leave it!
- Try to get behind the object and pull it out, rather than trying to grab it. The right-angle hook is perfect for that.
- The skin of the canal is exquisitely sensitive, try not to touch it.
}

◊section[#:id "conclusion"]{Conclusion}
It should be pretty clear by now that I love this piece of kit. I think it's fantastic for paediatricians: if I still worked in the hospital I'd have my O-Scope around my neck on every ward round. It's just as useful in the clinic.
